.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt X509_VERIFY_CERT 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm X509_build_chain ,
.Nm X509_verify_cert ,
.Nm X509_STORE_CTX_verify
.Nd build and verify X509 certificate chain
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/x509_vfy.h
.Bd -literal
 STACK_OF(X509) *X509_build_chain(X509 *target, STACK_OF(X509) *certs,
                                  X509_STORE *store, int with_self_signed,
                                  OSSL_LIB_CTX *libctx, const char *propq);
 int X509_verify_cert(X509_STORE_CTX *ctx);
 int X509_STORE_CTX_verify(X509_STORE_CTX *ctx);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr X509_build_chain 3
builds a certificate chain starting from
.Em target
using the optional list of intermediate CA certificates
.Em certs .
If
.Em store
is NULL it builds the chain as far down as possible, ignoring errors.
Else the chain must reach a trust anchor contained in
.Em store .
It internally uses a
.Sy X509_STORE_CTX
structure associated with the library context
.Em libctx
and property query string
.Em propq ,
both of which may be NULL. In case there is more than one possibility
for the chain, only one is taken.
.Pp
On success it returns a pointer to a new stack of (up_ref'ed)
certificates starting with
.Em target
and followed by all available intermediate certificates.
A self-signed trust anchor is included only if
.Em target
is the trust anchor of
.Em with_self_signed
is 1. If a non-NULL stack is returned the caller is responsible for
freeing it.
.Pp
The
.Xr X509_verify_cert 3
function attempts to discover and validate a certificate chain based on
parameters in
.Em ctx .
The verification context, of type
.Sy X509_STORE_CTX ,
can be constructed using
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_new 3
and
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_init 3 .
It usually includes a target certificate to be verified, a set of
certificates serving as trust anchors, a list of non-trusted
certificates that may be helpful for chain construction, flags such as
X509_V_FLAG_X509_STRICT, and various other optional components such as a
callback function that allows customizing the verification outcome.
A complete description of the certificate verification process is
contained in the
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
manual page.
.Pp
Applications rarely call this function directly but it is used by
OpenSSL internally for certificate validation, in both the S/MIME and
SSL/TLS code.
.Pp
A negative return value from
.Xr X509_verify_cert 3
can occur if it is invoked incorrectly, such as with no certificate set
in
.Em ctx ,
or when it is called twice in succession without reinitialising
.Em ctx
for the second call.
A negative return value can also happen due to internal resource
problems or because an internal inconsistency has been detected.
Applications must interpret any return value <= 0 as an error.
.Pp
The
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_verify 3
behaves like
.Xr X509_verify_cert 3
except that its target certificate is the first element of the list of
untrusted certificates in
.Em ctx
unless a target certificate is set explicitly.
.Pp
When the verification target is a raw public key, rather than a
certificate, both functions validate the target raw public key.
In that case the number of possible checks is significantly reduced.
The raw public key can be authenticated only via DANE TLSA records,
either locally synthesised or obtained by the application from DNS. Raw
public key DANE TLSA records may be added via
.Xr SSL_add_expected_rpk 3
or
.Xr SSL_dane_tlsa_add 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Xr X509_build_chain 3
returns NULL on error, else a stack of certificates.
.Pp
Both
.Xr X509_verify_cert 3
and
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_verify 3
return 1 if a complete chain can be built and validated, otherwise they
return 0, and in exceptional circumstances (such as malloc failure and
internal errors) they can also return a negative code.
.Pp
If a complete chain can be built and validated both functions return 1.
If the certificate must be rejected on the basis of the data available
or any required certificate status data is not available they return 0.
If no definite answer possible they usually return a negative code.
.Pp
On error or failure additional error information can be obtained by
examining
.Em ctx
using, for example,
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_get_error 3 .
Even if verification indicated success, the stored error code may be
different from X509_V_OK, likely because a verification callback
function has waived the error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_add_expected_rpk 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_dane_enable 3 ,
.Xr SSL_dane_tlsa_add 3 ,
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_new 3 ,
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_init 3 ,
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_init_rpk 3 ,
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_get_error 3
.Sh HISTORY
.Xr X509_build_chain 3
and
.Xr X509_STORE_CTX_verify 3
were added in OpenSSL 3.0.
